\h 1 Shemuel
\toc1 1 Shemuel
\toc2 1 Shemuel
\toc3 1 She
\mt1 1 Shemuel
\is1 Introduction
\ip This document tells about the people of the nation of Yisrael (called ‘Israel’ in older Bibles) at the time when Shemuel (commonly, but wrongly known as ‘Samuel’) was still their teacher through to when Sha'ul (commonly, but wrongly known as ‘Saul’) led them as their first king. It also reveals the reasons why Sha'ul lost his position as king and why God promised to replace him with David.
\ip Long ago before Yisrael had a king, the people regarded their god as their ruler, but eventually they came to the point of asking God to give them a king to lead them. Both the king and the people were subject to God's leading and expected to obey God's laws.
\ip The main theme of this document is showing how peace and prosperity came when the people obeyed God, and if not, it could lead to destruction. And then sometimes God used his people to apply his discipline to other people groups. It pays to note what's written in 2:30 that goes, “I will honour those who honour me, but those who despise me will be side-lined.”
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Shemuel as Yisrael's judge \ior 1:1–7:17\ior*
\io1 Sha'ul becomes king \ior 8:1–10:27\ior*
\io1 The first years of Sha'ul's reign \ior 11:1–15:35\ior*
\io1 David and Sha'ul \ior 16:1–30:31\ior*
\io1 The death of Sha'ul and his sons \ior 31:1-13\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Elkanah and family at Shiloh
\rem /s1 Elkanah and His Family; Samuel's birth and childhood; The Birth of Samuel; Samuel's Birth and Dedication; Elkanah and His Family at Shiloh
\p
\v 1 There was a man¦125380 named Elkanah¦125389 from¦125382 Ramatayim¦125384-Zofim¦125385 \add who lived\add* in the Efrayimite hill¦125386 country. (He was an Efrayimite—the son of Yeroham¦125392, son of Elihu¦125395,\x + \xo 1:1 \xt 1 Chr. 6:27,34.\x* son of Tohu¦125398, son of Zuf¦125401.)
\v 2 He¦125413 had two¦125405 wives¦125406: Hannah¦125409 (who had no children) and¦125404 Peninnah¦125412 (who had \add several\add* children).
\p
\v 3 \add ≈Every year\add* he¦125420 would¦125420 \add take his family\add* to Shiloh¦125431 to \add ≈offer sacrifices¦125428\add* to commander¦125430 Yahweh. Eli¦125436 was Yahweh's¦125429 priest¦125439 there, and his two¦125433 sons¦125434 Hofni¦125437 and Pinehas¦125438 \add also served there\add*.
\p
\v 4 on the day¦125443 that Elkanah¦125445 made his sacrifice¦125444, he would give¦125446 portions¦125453 \add of the cooked meat\add* to Peninnah¦125447 and to all¦125449 her¦125451 sons¦125451 and daughters¦125452,
\v 5 \add ≈but¦125455\add* he'd give¦125456 a double¦125459 portion¦125457 to Hannah¦125463 because¦125460 he loved¦125464 \add *her\add*, \add even though\add* Yahweh¦125465 had¦125466 \add ≈not enabled her to have children\add*.
\v 6 Then¦125469 her rival¦125470 would provoke¦125469 her and¦125469 \add ≈try to¦125475 make her angry\add*, because¦125476 Yahweh¦125479 hadn't \add ≈given her children\add*.
\v 7 And so¦125483 it \add ≈happened every year\add*. Every time they went to Yahweh's¦125490 \add ≈tent\add*, \add @Peninnah\add* ridiculed him, and \add @Hannah\add* would weep¦125493 and wouldn't eat¦125495.
\v 8 Then¦125497 Elkanah¦125499 would \add ≈ask\add* her, “Hannah¦125501, why are you crying? And why aren't¦125505 you eating¦125506? And why \add ≈are you so upset\add*? Isn't it¦125508 better \add ≈having me\add* than \add ≈having\add* ten¦125514 sons¦125515?”
\s1 Hannah and Eli
\rem /s1 Hannah and Eli; Hannah's Prayer for a Son
\p
\v 9 \add One time\add* Hannah¦125518 got up after eating and¦125517 drinking¦125523 at Shiloh¦125521. (Eli¦125524 the priest¦125525 was¦125526 sitting¦125526 on¦125527 the seat¦125529 near the entrance of Yahweh's¦125534 \add ≈tent\add*.)
