\v 12 \add ≈Now\add* Eli's¦126039 sons¦126038 were¦126040 \add ≈worthless¦126041 scoundrels\add* who didn't¦126042 obey Yahweh¦126046.
\v 13 It was the custom¦126048 of those priests¦126049 that when anyone was offering a sacrifice¦126056 and¦126048 as soon as the meat¦126062 was boiling¦126061, the priest's¦126060 servant¦126059 would come¦126058 along holding a three-pronged¦126064 fork¦126063
\v 14 and¦126069 stick it into the pot or pan. The¦126080 priest¦126082 would then¦126069 \add ≈claim\add* whatever the fork¦126080 brought¦126079 up. That's what they¦126085 did¦126085 to all¦126086 the Israelis who¦126089 came to Shiloh¦126091 \add to offer sacrifices\add*.
\v 15 Also, before¦126094 the fat¦126098 \add was removed to\add* burn, the priest's¦126102 servant¦126101 would say to the person¦126104 sacrificing¦126105, “Give¦126106 some meat to the priest—he doesn't want just boiled¦126115 meat \add all the time\add*—he'll take some raw¦126119 meat \add as well\add* to roast¦126108.”
\p
\v 16 But¦126121 if the person tried to say¦126121, “Once the fat¦126127 has all been burnt, then take whatever you want for¦126130 yourself¦126130,” then he'd \add ≈assert\add*, “No, you'd better give¦126140 it \add right\add* now¦126139 or I'll take it by¦126145 force¦126145.”
\v 17 And \add so\add* the¦126148 actions of¦126148 those young¦126149 men¦126149 were very¦126151 wicked in Yahweh's¦126155 mind because¦126156 they¦126157 \add just\add* treated¦126157 sacrifices to Yahweh with contempt¦126157.
