\p “\add Well,\add* there's just the youngest¦133168,” he \add ≈answered\add* “\add ≈but¦133158\add* you see, he's¦133167 \add out\add* \add ≈looking after\add* the flock.”\f + \fr 16:11 \ft We deliberately left the word ‘flock’ here¦133186 (rather than ‘sheep’), because¦133179 although young David is always thought of as ‘the shepherd boy’ in English culture, in the middle-East, \+bd goats\+bd* are probably a more important part of the flock than sheep.\f*
\p “Send¦133177 for him,” Shemuel \add ≈instructed\add* \add *him\add*, “because we¦133182 won't¦133180 \add ≈do anything else until¦133183 he¦133165 gets\add* here.”
\v 12 \add ≈So¦133188\add* he sent¦133188 for him and brought¦133189 him in. He¦133190 was¦133191 a good-looker with¦133192 beautiful¦133194 eyes¦133195 and red¦133191 colouring, and Yahweh¦133200 said¦133199, “\add ≈Stand up\add* \add and\add* anoint¦133202 him, because¦133203 \add ≈he's¦133206 the one\add*.”
