\p
\v 10 Then the next¦134669 day, an evil¦134674 spirit¦134671 from God¦134672 rushed¦134670 on¦134680 Sha'ul and \add even\add* in¦134680 the middle¦134680 of¦134680 the house¦134682 he \add started\add* prophesying¦134679. As David¦134683 was playing¦134684 near him (as¦134686 he did every day), Sha'ul was holding a spear¦134689
\v 11 and¦134694 he \add suddenly\add* hurled¦134694 \add *it\add*, saying¦134699, “I'll¦134700 \add ≈pin that David to\add* the wall¦134702.” \add ≈But¦134694\add* twice when he did that, David \add ≈managed to elude¦134703 him¦134705\add*.
\p
\v 12 \add ≈So¦134708\add* Sha'ul¦134709 \add ended up\add* afraid¦134708 of David¦134711 because¦134712 Yahweh¦134715 was with him¦134716, but¦134708 \add ≈was no longer with the king\add*.
\v 13 Then¦134721 Sha'ul¦134722 sent him \add ≈out of the room\add* and appointed¦134724 him as the commander¦134726 of a thousand¦134728 warriors, \add ≈so\add* he¦134729 came¦134730 and went¦134729 in¦134731 front¦134731 of the people¦134732.\f + \fr 18:13 \ft It's not entirely clear what the implications of this coming and going were (and similarly at the end of v16). It could easily refer to him returning successfully from military excursions.\f*
\v 14 Yahweh¦134742 was with David¦134736 and¦134735 he was succeeding¦134741 in \add ≈everything he did\add*.
\v 15 Sha'ul¦134746 \add ≈knew about his¦134753 successes¦134750 and¦134752 couldn't stand looking at him¦134753\add*,
\v 16 \add ≈but¦134755 all¦134755 the people of\add* Yisrael¦134757 and Yehudah¦134758 loved¦134759 David¦134762 because¦134763 \add ≈they observed his comings¦134767 and goings¦134766\add*.
\s1 David wins Mikal as his wife
