\p
\v 17 \add One day\add* Sha'ul told David¦134775, “Look, \add here's\add* my¦134777 oldest daughter¦134777 Merab¦134779—I'll give¦134781 her¦134780 to you for a wife. \add The only condition is that you\add* \add ≈become¦134786 a powerful warrior\add* for me and fight¦134792 Yahweh's¦134794 battles¦134793.” (Sha'ul had said¦134796 \add to himself\add*, “\add ≈I don't¦134797 need to hurt him¦134801 physically—I'll let¦134802 the Philistines¦134807 do that\add* \add for me\add*.”)
\v 18 “Who¦134815 am¦134816 I,” David¦134811 \add ≈asked\add*, “and¦134817 who¦134817 are¦134818 my relatives in my father's¦134820 clan¦134819 in Yisrael¦134821, that I should become the king's¦134826 son-in-law¦134825?”
\p
\v 19 \add ≈However,\add* when it¦134828 was the time¦134829 for Sha'ul¦134836 to give¦134830 his daughter¦134834 Merab¦134833 to David¦134837, she was given¦134839 to Adriel¦134840 the Meholathite¦134841 \add ≈to marry\add*.
