\v 27 \add ≈However,\add* the next¦136091 day¦136091 of¦136091 the \add ≈celebration\add* when David¦136096 was¦136094 still missing, Sha'ul¦136099 asked his¦136103 son Yonatan, “Why¦136104 didn't¦136105 Yishay's¦136110 son come¦136107 to¦136117 the meal yesterday¦136113 or today?”
\p
\v 28 “David¦136128 \add ≈begged\add* me¦136129 \add ≈to let him go to\add* Bethlehem,” Yonatan answered¦136121.
\v 29 “He¦136135 \add ≈requested\add*, ‘Please¦136137 \add ≈release me\add* because¦136138 our clan's¦136140 sacrifice¦136139 will be in the city¦136142, and my brother¦136147 has¦136144 \add ≈told¦136144 me to be there¦136160\add*. \add ≈So¦136135\add* now¦136148, if¦136149 I've¦136151 found¦136151 favour¦136152 in your¦136153 eyes¦136153, please¦136137 let¦136154 me slip¦136154 away¦136154 and \add ≈go¦136136 to\add* see¦136156 my brothers¦136159.’ \add ≈That's¦136156 why\add* he¦136165 hasn't come¦136165 to the king's¦136169 table¦136168.”
\p
\v 30 Sha'ul¦136175 \add ≈was furious at\add* Yonatan and \add ≈yelled at\add* him¦136178, “\add You\add* son of a perverse¦136181, rebellious¦136182 woman! Do¦136184 \add you think that\add* I don't¦136183 know¦136184 that you're \add ≈taking the side of\add* Yishay's¦136191 son? It's to your \add ≈detriment\add* and to the shame¦136193 of your mother's¦136195 nakedness¦136194,
\v 31 because as long as that¦136201 son¦136202 of Yishay¦136204 stays alive on this earth, you¦136210 and your¦136212 kingdom¦136212 will¦136210 never be¦136210 established¦136210. Now¦136216, send¦136214 \add men\add* and \add ≈arrest\add* him¦136216, because he's¦136222 \add ≈on his way to the grave\add*!”
\p
\v 32 “Why should he be¦136234 put¦136234 to¦136234 death¦136234?” Yonatan \add ≈retorted\add*. “What¦136235 \add wrong\add* has¦136236 he done¦136236?”
\p
\v 33 Then¦136238 Sha'ul¦136239 hurled¦136238 his spear¦136242 at him to \add ≈kill him\add*, and¦136238 Yonatan¦136246 knew¦136245 \add then\add* that¦136247 his father¦136252 had¦136249 \add already\add* \add ≈determined¦136249 to kill\add* David¦136256.
\v 34 Yonatan angrily got up¦136259 from¦136261 the table¦136262. He¦136268 didn't¦136266 eat¦136268 any food¦136273 that second¦136272 day¦136269 of¦136271 the \add ≈celebration\add*—he¦136275 was upset about David because¦136274 his father¦136281 had dishonoured him.\f + \fr 20:34 \ft It's not entirely clear who this ‘him’ was (as Sha'ul had seriously dishonoured Yonatan as well), so we've left it ambiguous (although David was certainly the last name mentioned).\f*
\v 35 \add ≈So it¦136284 was¦136284 that the following\add* morning¦136285, Yonatan went¦136286 out to the countryside¦136288 \add ≈at¦136285 the time¦136289 agreed\add* with David¦136290, taking a small¦136292 boy¦136291 \add with him\add*.
\v 36 “Run¦136297 \add ahead\add*—find¦136298 the arrows¦136302 that¦136303 I'm about¦136305 to shoot¦136305,” he told his boy. The boy ran¦136307 and¦136295 he shot¦136310 the arrow¦136311 to \add ≈go over his head\add*.
\v 37 The¦136315 boy went¦136314 to¦136316 where the arrow was, \add ≈but¦136314\add* Yonatan called¦136323 out, “Isn't the arrow \add ≈further past\add* you¦136330?”
\v 38 Then¦136333 he¦136333 \add ≈added\add*, “\add ≈Hurry up! Don't¦136339 mess around\add*.” \add ≈So¦136333\add* the boy¦136336 picked¦136342 up the arrow¦136347 and¦136333 brought it back to¦136350 his¦136352 master¦136352,
